<Subpage
  title="System-wide spell check on Windows, not just in the browser"
  description="Spell check only works in apps that build it in. Here is why, and how unflub checks and fixes spelling in every text field on your PC, and your text stays on it."
  crumb="Spell check in every app"
>
  <h1>System-wide spell check, not just in your browser</h1>
  <p class="lead">
    Your browser underlines typos. Your chat app might. Your note app, your design tool and that internal form probably do
    not. Here is why, and how system-wide autocorrect closes the gap.
  </p>

  <h2>Spell check and autocorrect are different jobs</h2>
  <p>
    <strong>Spell check</strong> finds a misspelled word and marks it, usually with a red underline. You still have to stop,
    right-click and pick a fix. <strong>Autocorrect</strong> fixes the word for you as soon as you finish it, the way a phone
    keyboard does. Spell check tells you about the problem; autocorrect makes it go away.
  </p>

  <h2>Why some apps check spelling and others do not</h2>
  <p>
    On Windows, every app has to wire up spell checking itself. Browsers and apps built on them use the Windows spell checker
    to draw red underlines. Office has its own. Many other apps never add it, and none of them fix the word for you.
  </p>
  <p>So the same typo is caught in one window and missed in the next, and even where it is caught, you fix it by hand.</p>

  <h2>How unflub works in every text field</h2>
  <p>
    unflub works at the keyboard instead of inside each app, so every text field gets the same treatment. When you finish a
    word with a space, punctuation or Enter, it decides whether the word is wrong and, if it is confident, swaps in the fix
    before your next key lands.
  </p>
  <ul>
    <li>
      <strong>Spotting typos:</strong> it can ask the same Windows spell checker your browser uses, and it carries its own
      dictionary so it behaves the same on every computer.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Picking the fix:</strong> it ranks candidates by how common the word is, which keys sit next to each other,
      swapped or doubled letters, and the word before.
    </li>
    <li>
      <strong>Knowing when to stop:</strong> ambiguous words are left alone, and so are code, links, file paths, password fields
      and the words you have taught it.
    </li>
    <li><strong>Undo:</strong> press Backspace right after a fix to get your word back. unflub remembers it.</li>
  </ul>
  <p>All of this runs on your computer. Nothing you type is sent anywhere.</p>

  <h2>Which apps it covers</h2>
  <p>
    Anywhere you can type text: chat and email apps, browsers and web apps, note apps, AI prompt boxes, office tools and code
    editors (where it only fixes the prose). Terminals and password managers are skipped by default, and you can add any app
    to the skip list.
  </p>
</Subpage>
